  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The provided text is an introductory editorial overview rather than an empirical study with specific quantitative measurements.",
      "what": "This special issue introduces a collection of research and practice-based papers focused on urban greening strategies to enhance public health, environmental quality, and social wellbeing. It frames 'living green' as an active, ongoing process of integrating nature into urban planning rather than treating vegetation as static infrastructure.",
      "why": "It establishes a multidisciplinary framework that connects ecological restoration directly with social and cultural health, helping planners move from merely adding green spaces to cultivating active, health-promoting urban ecosystems."
    }
